    // =====================================================================
    // DESIGN-PATTERN CONFORMANCE CRITIQUE (2026-07-19): 5 independent critics evaluated the 27
    // pattern-tagged CodeElements against their declared pattern (findings VERIFIED against the code).
    // The pure cores are exemplary (decide/mapPool/safe_rel_path/validateStatus/the serialized actor);
    // the gaps are (1) a real validation DEFECT, (2) bool-predicates that should be refined types,
    // (3) shells that still decide, (4) the sync FSMs orphaned + several IMPLICIT FSMs, (5) mislabeled
    // tags. Triaged to 3 OPEN follow-on actions (implement later, per the owner). "we'll implement in a bit."
    // =====================================================================
    part issueValidateFileMetaMtimeUnchecked : Issue { :>> id = "c0f1a5cc-e030-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4e030"; :>> title = "validateFileMeta asserts mtime:number in its return type but never validates it — a hostile server's non-numeric mtime passes and flows into base stat-stamping"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "DEFECT (Medium), surfaced by the parse-don't-validate conformance critic + VERIFIED. protocol.ts:49-57 validateFileMeta validates path/hash/size/version (asStr/asNum) + chunks (string[]) but has NO check for mtime, then returns `o as FileMeta` (line 56) — the FileMeta type (protocol.ts) asserts mtime:number. So a malformed/hostile server response with mtime:'evil' / NaN / missing passes validation and is cast to FileMeta; mtime flows into live base stat-stamping (reconcile localStat/stampStat paths). The exact parse-don't-validate hazard: the refined type promises a property the parser doesn't establish. Contrast validateStatus (protocol.ts:61-72), which CONSTRUCTS a fresh normalized value (the true exemplar). FIX: add asNum(m.mtime, 'mtime') and construct-don't-cast (build + return a fresh FileMeta). Severity Medium (untrusted-input validation hole; the client already re-hashes downloaded CONTENT so this is metadata-integrity, not content-loss). discoveredInField=false (critique)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "patternConformanceHardening"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    part issueBoolPredicatesNoRefinedType : Issue { :>> id = "c0f1a5cc-e031-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4e031"; :>> title = "Security bool-predicates (safe_name / isSafeVaultPath / isInsecureRemote) return bool not a refined type, forcing the checked property to be re-asserted at ~20 / 5 / 4 call sites (parse-don't-validate gap)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "parse-don't-validate conformance critic (verified). The weakness clusters in bool safety-predicates whose un-typed result forces defensive re-checking everywhere — exactly what the pattern eliminates: (1) safe_name (users.rs:28-37) is re-checked at ~20 call sites (admin.rs x10, state.rs x7, api.rs/vaults.rs/ws.rs/auth.rs) and DOUBLE-checked on the register write-path (auth.rs:136 then users.rs:133; state.rs:272 even comments 'the admin route already validated; defensive') — a name that reaches the FS as a path segment relies on every site remembering to call it; highest ROI. (2) isSafeVaultPath (pathsafe.ts:14-32) re-checked at 5 client I/O sites (main.ts write/read/exists/write/remove). (3) isInsecureRemote (connstr.ts:21-27) independently re-parses the raw URL via new URL() at 4 transport.ts sites, alongside (not consuming) normalizeServer's parse — shotgun parsing. FIX: a SafeName newtype (only ctor parses) taken by store keys/ns_dir/handlers; a branded SafeVaultPath taken by VaultIo read/write/remove (unchecked path → compile error); fold the insecure verdict into a refined ServerOrigin from normalizeServer. safe_rel_path (vault.rs, Option<PathBuf>, canonical round-trip, result trusted) is the reference parser to model on. Severity Medium (security-relevant reliance on human vigilance; no present bug). discoveredInField=false."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "patternConformanceHardening"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    part issueFunctionalCoreShellsReDecide : Issue { :>> id = "c0f1a5cc-e032-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4e032"; :>> title = "The reconcile shells (reconcileOne/reconcileMergeOrConflict/reconcilePath) carry still-pure decision logic that should be lifted into explicit pure values (functional-core boundary too narrow)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "functional-core/imperative-shell conformance critic (verified). decide() is an EXEMPLARY pure total truth table (purity deliberately protected via requireRemote). But the three shells re-decide: (1) reconcileOne (reconcile.ts:780-947) delegates the 3-way call to decide() then adds a SECOND layer of largely-pure decision logic in the shell — config re-adjudication (848-858), delete gating on hasTombstone/guardDelete/guardRemoteDelete (897,910,921,929), size gate (792-798). Lift into a pure finalize(action, facts)→Effect (resolve confirmedAbsent/exists to booleans in the shell first); the switch then only executes Effects. (2) reconcileMergeOrConflict (700-760) uses the pure kernels (merge3/sameIgnoringEol/isMergeable) but interleaves outcome selection with I/O (partly inherent) — compute a pure MergePlan value, shell executes writes. (3) reconcilePath (617-657) inlines the delete-guard denominator/wouldDelete (626-641), duplicating runDeleteGuard (220-226, the B1 drift) — extract a shared perPassDeleteGuard(d, manifest) helper used by all three entry points. Net: widen the property-testable pure boundary. Severity Medium (design/testability; no correctness bug). discoveredInField=false."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "patternConformanceHardening"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    part issueStateMachineOrphanedAndImplicit : Issue { :>> id = "c0f1a5cc-e033-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4e033"; :>> title = "The 2 tagged sync FSMs are correct but DEAD (only the test uses them); the live operational machine is a hybrid, and ~4 IMPLICIT FSMs (WS lifecycle, engine, reconcile-mode, config-adjudication) are boolean-soup that should be explicit"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "actor/state-machine conformance critic (verified by grep + read). This answers 'why only 2 state machines?': (a) syncstate::transition + SyncMachine (syncstate.ts) are CORRECT total (State,Event)→State machines but ORPHANED — imported only by client/test/syncstate.test.ts; the operational machine migrated to syncengine.ts, whose header says the old FSM 'only ever drove the status LIGHT'. So the 2 tagged FSMs model nothing live. (b) The LIVE engine (SyncEngine, syncengine.ts) is a HYBRID: an explicit EngineState enum + 3 aux booleans (connected/retrying/running) + IMPERATIVE transitions in handle() — not a total transition table. (c) IMPLICIT FSMs that should be explicit, by value: D1 WS/transport lifecycle (main.ts spinUpWs/startWsLiveness/close-handler — realtimeConnected/opened/`ws!==ws`/poll-cadence/wsLivenessTimer encode no-socket/dialing/open/half-open-stale/closed-redial/closed-reconnect, with a documented history of race patches CONC-R2#4/#6/CONC-R3#1/R11#7 → strongest case for an explicit TransportState machine); D4 the engine hybrid; D2 doReconcileAll scan-mode (4 booleans → 4 modes); D3 config-path adjudication (array-membership-as-state). The (serialized-actor) SyncEngine.pump/handle ARE strong (real queue, run-to-completion, owned state) — the FSM issue is the transition MODELING, not the actor. Resolve via stateMachineConsolidation (delete-or-reunify the orphans; make the implicit machines explicit, model = decide()). Severity Medium (clarity/testability + a race-patch-prone lifecycle; no present bug). discoveredInField=false."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "stateMachineConsolidation"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    part issuePatternTagsMislabeled : Issue { :>> id = "c0f1a5cc-e034-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4e034"; :>> title = "5 CodeElement design-pattern tags are mislabeled/overstated (+ a stale design-patterns.md exemplar claim) — the registry's own honesty"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Cross-cutting registry-accuracy finding from the conformance critics (verified). Five `pattern` tags don't match what the code does: ceContentStorePut is tagged singleWriter but is deliberately NOT single-writer (content-addressed immutable store; concurrent same-hash puts are safe by construction) → content-addressed-immutable; ceResolveConfigConflict is tagged command but reifies nothing (a direct async fn branching on a choice discriminant) → strategy; ceSharesAuthorized is tagged makeIllegalStatesUnrepresentable but is a runtime bool decision (the illegal state IS representable) → functionalCore/fail-closed-authz (only the Perm/Access two-enum split is a real make-illegal win); ceMergeEnabledPlugins tagged makeIllegalStatesUnrepresentable but the grow-only invariant is an algorithmic Set-union over string→string, not type-enforced → functionalCore+parseDontValidate; ceIsolatedPass tagged boundedPool but its bound is delegated to mapPool/FILE_CONCURRENCY → error-isolation-loop. Plus a DOC drift: .engine/docs/design-patterns.md:15 cites validateChanges/validateFileMeta as parseDontValidate exemplars, but both cast-not-construct and validateFileMeta has the mtime hole (issueValidateFileMetaMtimeUnchecked) — the true exemplars are safe_rel_path + validateStatus. Correcting these (with a DesignPattern enum extension where needed) keeps the descriptive-tag registry trustworthy. Severity Low (accuracy; the tags are descriptive). discoveredInField=false."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "patternRegistryReview"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    part issueUntaggedElementsPatternReview : Issue { :>> id = "c0f1a5cc-e035-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4e035"; :>> title = "The 142 untagged CodeElements (pattern=none) were never reviewed for a pattern they SHOULD follow — likely under-counting FSMs and other proven shapes"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-19"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-raised (2026-07-19): only 27 of 169 CodeElements carry a design pattern and only 2 are state machines, which under-counts given how much of the code is 'functions that operate over pieces of data and decide what state is represented' — FSMs fit that shape strongly. The `pattern` tag was applied opportunistically during the audit, so most elements read `none` without a deliberate check for whether a proven pattern APPLIES. ACTION: pass over the 142 untagged elements (pattern=none / no attr) and tag where a pattern SHOULD apply, recognizing the recurring SHAPE — state+events→decision ⇒ FSM; untrusted-input→trusted-downstream ⇒ parseDontValidate; concurrent-mutation ⇒ singleWriter; pure-decision-over-inputs ⇒ functionalCore — NOT just accepting `none`. A pattern earns a tag when the code REPEATEDLY solves the problem the pattern names (that is why they are 'patterns' — strong, proven answers to recurring shapes). Seeded by the implicit FSMs the conformance critique already surfaced (issueStateMachineOrphanedAndImplicit: the WS-lifecycle, engine, reconcile-mode, config-adjudication machines). The output raises the tagged-pattern coverage honestly + feeds stateMachineConsolidation. Severity Low (registry completeness / design insight). discoveredInField=false."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "patternRegistryReview"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from patternConformanceHardening to issueValidateFileMetaMtimeUnchecked;
    #Resolves dependency from patternConformanceHardening to issueBoolPredicatesNoRefinedType;
    #Resolves dependency from patternConformanceHardening to issueFunctionalCoreShellsReDecide;
    #Resolves dependency from stateMachineConsolidation to issueStateMachineOrphanedAndImplicit;
    #Resolves dependency from patternRegistryReview to issuePatternTagsMislabeled;
    #Resolves dependency from patternRegistryReview to issueUntaggedElementsPatternReview;

    // =====================================================================
    // UNTAGGED PATTERN REVIEW executed 2026-07-19 (7 independent reviewers over the 142 untagged
    // CodeElements). Outcome: 56 already-conform tags APPLIED to the registry (tagged 27→83); the
    // audit had badly under-counted proven patterns (store writes=singleWriter, boundary parsers=
    // parseDontValidate, pure cores=functionalCore, the HTTP adapter, RAII guards, the serialized
    // actor, the UI templateMethod modals). Reviewers correctly declined to force-fit (login flow,
    // LoginThrottle, thin handlers, GC helpers = honest `none`). The SHOULD-ADOPT findings below are
    // the refactor work the review surfaced (recorded, implement later). The mislabel retags + the
    // doc fix remain under patternRegistryReview (some need a DesignPattern enum extension → a CR).
    // =====================================================================
    part issueVaultLifecycleImplicitFsm : Issue { :>> id = "c0f1a5cc-e036-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4e036"; :>> title = "Vault's corrupt + dangling_recovery bool pair is an IMPLICIT lifecycle FSM (Ok/Corrupt/DanglingRecovery) that can even represent an illegal state — should be an explicit enum VaultState"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Surfaced by the untagged-pattern review (server-durability reviewer, verified). Vault carries the lifecycle as TWO booleans — corrupt + dangling_recovery (vault.rs) — driven by open()→verify_and_gc()→reindex() and read by api::ensure_ready. The states are Ok / Corrupt[unreadable | empty-with-data] / DanglingRecovery[intact index, missing blob], plus an Unsupported→hard-refuse exit. The bool PAIR can represent the ILLEGAL combo (corrupt=false, dangling_recovery=true), and the transitions are imperative flag-sets across three methods. This is a genuine implicit state machine (distinct from the client sync-lifecycle FSMs in issueStateMachineOrphanedAndImplicit — this one is server durability). FIX: replace the pair with an `enum VaultState { Ok, Corrupt, DanglingRecovery }` (makeIllegalStatesUnrepresentable), with verify_and_gc/reindex as total guarded transitions and ensure_ready reading the enum exhaustively — modeled on decide()'s enumerated table. Folds into stateMachineConsolidation (which broadens to sync + vault lifecycle FSMs). Severity Medium (clarity + an illegal representable state; no present bug — the flags are set correctly today). discoveredInField=false."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "stateMachineConsolidation"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    part issuePatternUntaggedShouldAdopt : Issue { :>> id = "c0f1a5cc-e037-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4e037"; :>> title = "Untagged-review should-adopt refactors: ChunkHash newtype, conflictCopyIfRaced discriminated union, parseSettings boundary, release_open_lock Drop guard, audit action/outcome newtypes"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "The untagged-pattern review (7 reviewers, verified) found a handful of elements whose SHAPE fits a proven pattern but that implement it messily — refactor targets (each with a fail-first/behavior-preserving test when done): (1) chunkstore::is_valid_hash — a bool validator re-run in has/get/remove/touch/put; a `ChunkHash` newtype parsed ONCE at the boundary makes the raw &str hash unrepresentable downstream (parseDontValidate/newtype). (2) reconcile.ts::conflictCopyIfRaced — returns `string|null` but smuggles a MAGIC sentinel (' config-race', a leading-NUL non-path) through the path-valued channel; a discriminated union `{kind:'copy';path} | {kind:'config-race'} | null` precludes a caller conflating the marker with a real copy path (makeIllegalStatesUnrepresentable). (3) main.ts::loadSettings — hardens untrusted data.json at the boundary but ad-hoc (Object.assign + per-field Array.isArray); formalize a `parseSettings(raw): NewLiveSyncSettings` parser (parseDontValidate). (4) state.rs::release_open_lock — manually reaped on EVERY vault()/purge_vault return path (4+ sites, easy to forget); a Drop-based guard removes the release-on-all-exits hazard (raiiGuard) — note the reap is strong_count-based so the Drop needs the same check (low-confidence). (5) audit.rs — action/outcome 'closed catalogs' are &str consts, so the signature accepts arbitrary strings; newtypes/enums would enforce the catalog (newtype/makeIllegalStatesUnrepresentable, low). Severity Low (design/clarity; no present bugs). discoveredInField=false."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "patternConformanceHardening"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from stateMachineConsolidation to issueVaultLifecycleImplicitFsm;
    #Resolves dependency from patternConformanceHardening to issuePatternUntaggedShouldAdopt;

    // ===== Release-discipline blind spot (surfaced 2026-07-19 when the owner questioned why 99 commits
    // sat unreleased despite the D0012 release-verify guard). D0047: logged as a tracked Issue + slated for
    // a permanent automated control (releaseCurrencyGuard; the guard itself is a process CHANGE pending a
    // Change-Request Decision + acceptance). =====
    part issueStatusLightFlicker : Issue { :>> id = "c0f1a5cc-e039-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4e039"; :>> title = "Status light flits 'Fully synced' <-> 'Syncing... checking for changes' ~1x/sec — a transient reconcile (a check) is painted as a STATE (UX/human-factors defect)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-28"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported on a real device: the status light oscillates between 'Fully synced' and 'Syncing... checking for changes' about once a second. Root cause: a routine poll / config re-hash / path event flips the engine phase idle->syncing(syncPending=0)->idle in well under a second, and statusDisplay (main.ts:965) paints syncing-with-0-pending as 'Syncing... checking for changes' — so a transient CHECK is rendered as a STATE ('checking for changes is a state transition, not a state'). FIX: a debounced DISPLAY fsm (statuslight.ts) distinct from the engine FSM — the visible 'Syncing...' state requires syncPending>0 AND persistence past a short debounce, so a 0-pending or sub-second reconcile never paints and the light holds its steady state; 'checking for changes' is removed from statusDisplay; a render-dedupe skips identical repaints. Pure reducer + unit tests; behaviour otherwise unchanged. Severity Low (cosmetic/human-factors; no data effect). discoveredInField=true (owner, real device — the first field-discovered issue vs the critique-found set)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "statusLightFlickerFsm"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    part issueReleaseCurrencyBlindSpot : Issue { :>> id = "c0f1a5cc-e038-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4e038"; :>> title = "check-release/verify-released (D0012) only assert the MANIFEST's version is released — unreleased SHIPPABLE commits piling up on main (manifest left at an already-released version) are invisible"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-19"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "The owner asked why 99 commits had accumulated on main since 1.6.0 with no release, despite D0012 adding check-release.mjs + verify-released.yml specifically to make 'released' a checkable assertion. Root cause: those checks assert only that MANIFEST.json's version has a matching tag + published GitHub release — a condition SATISFIED the entire time, because the manifest sat at 1.6.0 (which WAS released) while main drifted 99 commits (48 of them touching shippable client/server/manifest paths; 52 engine/tracking/docs-only) past it. So D0012 catches 'bumped-but-never-tagged' (the 0.10.4 miss) but is BLIND to 'never-bumped-so-work-accrues-unreleased' — a distinct drift. This violated D0002 ('every coherent set of committed, green changes ships as a version increment') for ~a week across the mutation/critique/pattern sittings, undetected. FIX (releaseCurrencyGuard): a repo-local scripts/check-release-currency.mjs (mirroring D0012's out-of-keel-scope pattern) that counts main commits since the last release tag touching SHIPPABLE paths only (so tracking/engine/docs commits don't false-positive), WARNs at >=1 and FAILs past a threshold, wired into verify-released.yml. Severity Medium (a process-control gap that already caused a real week-long release lapse; no user-facing defect). discoveredInField=false."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "releaseCurrencyGuard"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from releaseCurrencyGuard to issueReleaseCurrencyBlindSpot;
    #Resolves dependency from statusLightFlickerFsm to issueStatusLightFlicker;

    // ===== Embedded-timestamp conflict noise (owner-reported field pain, 2026-07-29). D0077: triaged to
    // the embeddedTimestampMetadata sprint as its resolver. =====
    part issueTimestampConflictNoise : Issue { :>> id = "d7c18001-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d40081"; :>> title = "Sync raises conflicts/merges for timestamp-only frontmatter differences — the whole file is hashed, so any updated: bump reads as a content change"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-29"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported on real devices: 'wayy too many diff conflicts that are just time metadata with no file changes otherwise.' Root cause: change detection hashes the ENTIRE file byte-for-byte (reconcile.ts sha256hex), frontmatter included, with zero frontmatter awareness — so when a note's embedded modified/created timestamp differs across devices (a copy, a re-open, or an editor-event plugin re-stamp) while the body is identical, the hashes differ and decide() routes it to merge / conflict-copy. FIX (embeddedTimestampMetadata sprint): SelfSync owns the updated/created fields and compares by a NORMALIZED content hash that masks the managed timestamp keys (git-model content-addressing), so a timestamp-only difference is a non-event; a spurious local bump is reverted-not-pushed, and a genuine timestamp-only cross-device diff auto-resolves keeping the older updated / earliest created — no conflict copy. Severity Medium (no data loss, but a chronic false-conflict UX defect). discoveredInField=true (owner, real devices)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "embeddedTimestampMetadata"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from embeddedTimestampMetadata to issueTimestampConflictNoise;

    // Deferred findings from the pre-release 3-lens adversarial critique of embeddedTimestampMetadata
    // (independent critics, 2026-07-29). The ship-blockers (F1 non-md corruption, F2 cross-device churn,
    // F3 created=now on the edit path, F4 CRLF, F5 quoted dates) were fixed before release; these four
    // lower-severity items are deferred to a v-next hardening pass. Triaged to embeddedTimestampHardening.
    part issueTimestampHardeningFollowups : Issue { :>> id = "d7c18002-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d40082"; :>> title = "Embedded-timestamp v-next hardening: honor manual managed-field edits (F9), scan-skip vs external mtime-preserving tools (F12), DST-correct offset labels (F14), >1MiB churn-killer gap (F15)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-29"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Deferred (non-ship-blocking) findings from the embeddedTimestampMetadata pre-release critique: F9 (MEDIUM) the copy/re-stamp revert blind-restores base.text, so a user's manual correction of a managed created/updated is reverted every sync — should reconcile the managed fields (honor an earlier/legit value) instead; F12 (MED-LOW) driving FS mtime to the embedded updated weakens the scan-skip's 'a write bumps mtime' premise, so a body-only change by an external mtime-preserving tool (rsync -a) could be missed until a full scan — niche mixed-sync-tool setups; F14 (LOW) formatIsoOffset uses the CURRENT tz offset for a historically-stamped time, mislabeling by the DST delta (instant still correct, only the human offset is off); F15 (LOW/MED) a >1MiB managed note whose base text is dropped (MAX_BASE_TEXT_BYTES) skips the churn-killer revert and gets a spurious updated=now on a mere copy (bounded — converges after one push via persisted normHash). discoveredInField=false (critique-found). MOOTED 2026-08-01 by timestampIgnoreRedesign (1.9.0): all four findings targeted the 'SelfSync owns created/updated' code path — the copy/re-stamp revert (F9/F15), driving FS mtime to the embedded updated (F12), and formatIsoOffset (F14) — ALL of which the redesign DELETED (SelfSync now writes no note timestamps; identity masks timestamp-valued keys instead). With no stamping/revert/fs-mtime-drive code left, none of F9/F12/F14/F15 has a subject. Resolver repointed from the never-started embeddedTimestampHardening action to the completed redesign."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "timestampIgnoreRedesign"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from timestampIgnoreRedesign to issueTimestampHardeningFollowups;

    // Shipped-1.7.0 data-corruption found by the embeddedTimestampMetadata pre-release critique + VERIFIED
    // against the code; fixed in 1.7.1 (frontmatterParserSafety). D0047: the fix carries a permanent guard.
    part issueFrontmatterBlockScalarCorruption : Issue { :>> id = "d7c18003-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d40083"; :>> title = "1.7.0 frontmatter parser corrupts block-scalar notes (indented --- false-closes) + rewrites BOM/EOL/key-order on stamp"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-29"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Found by the pre-release 3-lens critique and VERIFIED against shipped 1.7.0: the line-based split() closed the block at the FIRST line trimming to '---', so an indented '---' inside a YAML block scalar (description: | ... '  ---') false-closed the frontmatter and DEMOTED real keys (title, tags) into the body — silent, permanent corruption, written to disk and pushed. Separately setManagedValue stripped a leading BOM, converted CRLF->LF, and reordered keys, while the enable-confirm falsely claimed 'won't touch your other frontmatter'. Reachable for any user who ENABLED embedded timestamps (default off, limited exposure) editing a note with a block-scalar-containing-'---'. FIX (1.7.1): col-0-only fence detection (block-scalar-safe) + surgical writes preserving BOM/EOL/key-order/all non-managed content, with block-scalar/BOM/CRLF/order/round-trip tests as the permanent guard. Severity High (silent data corruption in shipped code). discoveredInField=false (critique-found, before field amplification)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "frontmatterParserSafety"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    #Resolves dependency from frontmatterParserSafety to issueFrontmatterBlockScalarCorruption;

    // Owner-reported after 1.7.0: enabling the feature did nothing observable. Fixed in 1.8.0 (timestampBackfill).
    part issueTimestampEnableInert : Issue { :>> id = "d7c18004-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d40084"; :>> title = "Enabling embedded timestamps did nothing visible — feature was lazy (stamped only on next edit), so a settled vault saw no effect + no kickoff"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-29"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported after 1.7.0: 'embed created/updated timestamps doesn't make it clear when it's actually processing — it doesn't seem to be doing.' The feature stamped a note only on its next GENUINE EDIT; an already-synced/settled vault took the in-sync path and was never stamped, so flipping the toggle produced zero observable effect and there was no instantiation/kickoff. FIX (timestampBackfill, 1.8.0): a convergence pass on enable / policy-change / settled-launch that brings every in-scope .md note to compliance (seed from OS metadata, normalize non-canonical — instant-based, never bumps), paced + resumable via a per-vault marker/cursor, reversible via a conflict-copy for any not-cleanly-conformable note, gated by an informed COUNTED confirm with restore-signature detection; third-party alias keys masked in identity (no Linter re-stamp storm); Templates folder excluded. Severity Medium (usability — the feature appeared inert). discoveredInField=true (owner)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "timestampBackfill"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from timestampBackfill to issueTimestampEnableInert;

    // Owner-reported regression from the 1.6.2 flicker fix; fixed in 1.8.1 (statusHangsOnSyncing).
    part issueStatusHangsOnSyncing : Issue { :>> id = "d7c18005-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d40085"; :>> title = "Status card hangs on 'Syncing...' — statusText() rendered the RAW engine phase, bypassing the light's 0-pending collapse"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-29"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported after the 1.6.2 flicker fix: the status card 'just hangs on Syncing' whether or not an edit is made, and shows Syncing at launch. A device probe proved the ribbon light FSM was CORRECT (shown=idle) while enginePhase=syncing (a 0-pending reconcile at rest) — so the visible 'Syncing...' came from the settings status card, which renders statusText() = the RAW engine.phase(), bypassing the effectiveLightPhase collapse (syncing+0pending -> idle) the light already applied. The two surfaces disagreed. FIX (1.8.1): extract the collapse into a pure syncstate.effectivePhase(phase, syncPending) and route BOTH effectiveLightPhase AND statusText through it, so every surface shows 'Fully synced' when nothing is pending. Also cleaned a raw NUL byte accidentally shipped in the 1.8.0 backfill sentinel (source now uses an escaped sentinel). Severity Low (cosmetic/human-factors; no data effect). discoveredInField=true (owner, real device)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "statusHangsOnSyncing"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from statusHangsOnSyncing to issueStatusHangsOnSyncing;

    // ===== 1.9.0 timestamp-ignore redesign (2026-07-30): field damage from the 1.7-1.8 "SelfSync owns the
    // field" approach on a real COPIED multi-device vault, + a SEPARATE CRLF root cause. All resolved by
    // timestampIgnoreRedesign (SelfSync stops WRITING timestamps; identity ignores volatile diffs). =====
    part issueTimestampFabricatedCreated : Issue { :>> id = "d7c18006-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d40086"; :>> title = "Embedded-timestamp backfill FABRICATED created dates — a copied vault's ctime=copy-time was seeded as created:now"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-30"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported on the real vault C:\\notes\\ASI - Work: 'how is it making new files that are created now, when they clearly weren't (their original creation date isn't being respected).' Root cause: the 1.8.0 backfill seeded a missing created from OS metadata (ctime/mtime); the vault is a COPY, and Windows resets file-creation time to copy-time, so seedValues read ctime≈now and stamped created:<today>. detectRestoreSignature (a 5s bucket) was too narrow to notice a slow multi-file copy and suppress the seed. ~22 survivors ended at a fabricated created:2026-07-08 (an earlier Win32 backfill) + others at today; one note has created AFTER updated (provably fabricated). FIX (timestampIgnoreRedesign, 1.9.0): SelfSync writes NO timestamps at all, so it can never fabricate one. Existing fabricated dates are irrecoverable for a real subset (no git; copy-contaminated birth time) and are REPORTED, never invented (the cleanup is delete-only). Severity High (silent wrong data written into user notes). discoveredInField=true."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "timestampIgnoreRedesign"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    part issueTimestampBackfillConflictFlood : Issue { :>> id = "d7c18007-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d40087"; :>> title = "Embedded-timestamp backfill flooded the vault with timestamp-only conflict copies (planBackfillItem needsCopy true for every no-frontmatter note)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-30"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported: 'tons of conflict files that show no difference either... on most of these conflict files the only differences I observe are in the timestamps, which we were explicitly supposed to ignore.' Root cause: planBackfillItem computed needsCopy = normalizedContent(original) !== normalizedContent(conformed); for a note with NO frontmatter, conform ADDS a ---...--- block, so the two normalized strings always differ -> needsCopy=true -> a reversible conflict copy for essentially every plain note. On the real vault this produced 33 live conflict copies (32 timestamp/EOL-only + 1 real). FIX (timestampIgnoreRedesign, 1.9.0): the whole backfill (and its needsCopy path) is DELETED — SelfSync never conforms/writes, so no copies are spawned; a timestamp-only cross-device diff is resolved by identity masking with no copy. The 32 existing spurious copies were cleaned up (delete-only, reversible external backup). Severity High (mass spurious conflict copies obscuring real ones). discoveredInField=true."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "timestampIgnoreRedesign"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    part issueCrlfFalsePositiveChanges : Issue { :>> id = "d7c18008-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d40088"; :>> title = "CRLF/BOM-only differences are flagged as content changes — normalizedHash short-circuited to the RAW hash for a note without frontmatter (a SEPARATE root cause from timestamps)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-30"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported: 'TONS of false positives that incorrectly identify changes when none really exists (CR/LF issues?).' An independent design-fit critic confirmed this is a DISTINCT root cause from the timestamp noise: normalizedHash (frontmatter.ts) returned the RAW byte hash for any note WITHOUT frontmatter (hasFrontmatter false -> sha256hex(bytes)), so EOL/BOM normalization never ran on plain notes and a CRLF↔LF re-save read as a content change; and even for notes with frontmatter the EOL normalization was gated behind the (feature-on) path. FIX (timestampIgnoreRedesign, 1.9.0): content identity ALWAYS normalizes EOL/BOM/trailing-newline for ALL text files, independent of the timestamp feature (the no-frontmatter short-circuit removed), and a cosmetic-only local change is recognized in-sync (not pushed) via the post-decide normHash override. Guard: the CRLF-only-not-pushed reconcile test + plain-note EOL identity tests. Severity Medium (chronic false-conflict/churn UX defect; no data loss). discoveredInField=true."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "timestampIgnoreRedesign"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from timestampIgnoreRedesign to issueTimestampFabricatedCreated;
    #Resolves dependency from timestampIgnoreRedesign to issueTimestampBackfillConflictFlood;
    #Resolves dependency from timestampIgnoreRedesign to issueCrlfFalsePositiveChanges;

    // ===== Field-reported 2026-07-30 (investigated, NOT yet fixed) — four follow-up defects. =====
    part issuePluginSyncStaleServerState : Issue { :>> id = "d7c18009-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d40089"; :>> title = "A plugin the user removed still shows as 'synced' — server plugin FILES are never pruned and community-plugins.json is grow-only, so a removal never propagates"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-30"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported: 'update-time-on-edit shows up in the synced community plugins' though not installed; 'there should be absolute parity here.' Root cause (verified): (A) the synced-plugins list is a UNION of local manifests + getServerPluginIds(); getServerPluginIds is derived by regex over EVERY remote file path (reconcile.ts onRemotePlugins), so it = every plugin with ANY file on the server. (B) removal never propagates: setPluginSync(id,false) only drops the id from pluginAllow (deletes nothing); then shouldSync=false -> the accepts predicate skips those paths ENTIRELY (no pull, no base, NO delete-remote), so the server folder lingers forever. (C) community-plugins.json is a deliberately grow-only union (mergeEnabledPluginsJson: enables propagate, disables do not, reconcile.ts:806-807), so the id is re-added every sync and never removed — why the vault still lists it enabled. FIX (pluginSyncRemovalPropagation): UI split (available-to-adopt vs synced) + a sanctioned removal channel (delete-remote for the plugin folder + a removal op the enabled-list merge honors) — a CHANGE to the catastrophe-proof-asymmetric merge, needs a Decision. Severity Medium. discoveredInField=true."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "pluginSyncRemovalPropagation"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    part issuePluginSyncFolderIdNotName : Issue { :>> id = "d7c1800a-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4008a"; :>> title = "Synced plugins display their folder id (update-time-on-edit) instead of the display name (Update time on edit) when not installed locally"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-30"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported: 'when plugins are synced, they indicate their file/folder name instead of display name, e.g. update-time-on-edit instead of Update time on edit; other names are more severe but that's the pattern.' Root cause (verified, settings.ts:495 + :449): the row name is manifests[id]?.name || id, and manifests = this.app.plugins.manifests = LOCAL installed manifests only. A server-only (not-installed-here) plugin has no local manifest, so name is undefined and it falls back to the folder id. The real name IS on the server (in the plugin's synced manifest.json) but onRemotePlugins (reconcile.ts) throws the content away, keeping only regex-extracted ids. FIX (pluginSyncDisplayName): carry the manifest 'name' from the server (lazy/cached for not-installed ids); display manifests[id]?.name || serverName(id) || id. Severity Low (cosmetic/legibility). discoveredInField=true."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "pluginSyncDisplayName"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    part issueVaultChangeSkipsTransition : Issue { :>> id = "d7c1800b-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4008b"; :>> title = "A vault change via the setup wizard skips the merge/direction transition and reconciles against a STALE base — silent overwrite risk (data-integrity)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-30"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported after a reconnect: 'i reconnected but didn't get the modal i expected (merge / keep device's / synced's)... joining a new vault needs some kind of transition logic, unless the re-logging in really did intelligently decide it wasn't needed.' Investigation (verified): a plain reconnect to the SAME vault correctly skips the modal (base intact -> normal incremental resume — WORKING AS DESIGNED, the modal is not broken). BUT a real latent GAP: SetupWizardModal.finish() (setupwizard.ts:255-279) writes st.vaultId directly WITHOUT setting pendingSwitch, WITHOUT clearing the base, and WITHOUT any direction modal, then reconnect() -> doConnect runs reconcileFull over the OLD vault's base. The base is a single global store cleared only by switchTo (reconcile.ts:1167). So changing vaults through the wizard (manually picking another vault, or the previously-synced vault no longer existing -> fallback to vaults[0]) makes decide() run against a FOREIGN base; the B===L->pull branch then SILENTLY OVERWRITES a local file whose new-vault copy differs — no conflict-copy, no prompt. Reachable from the user's exact scenario (token idle-expiry -> freshLogin -> openSetup -> wizard). FIX (vaultChangeTransitionGuard): route a wizard vault-change through switchToVault; defense-in-depth tag the base with owner/vaultId and force a switch/clear when it mismatches. Severity High (silent data overwrite on a vault change). discoveredInField=true."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "vaultChangeTransitionGuard"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    part issueSessionIdleReloginFriction : Issue { :>> id = "d7c1800c-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4008c"; :>> title = "Credentials 'stop working' until reconnect (a silent idle-logout) — token-only default + 30-min server idle timeout, correct but surprising and not clearly surfaced"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-30"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported: 'my credentials stopped working in selfsync but worked when I reconnected. would something recent have corrupted the login state?' Investigation (verified): NOT corruption. SelfSync defaults to TOKEN-ONLY (no stored password on disk, IA.3.5.10) and the server IDLE-EXPIRES a session after 30 min of no activity (DEFAULT_IDLE_TTL_SECS, AC.3.1.11; tokens otherwise persist across restarts, hashed, in .tokens.json). A device whose app was closed/asleep >30 min has its token idle-expire; with no stored password freshLogin can't silently renew, so it asks to reconnect — re-entering the password minted a fresh token. Working as designed, but the friction is real for a personal vault and the 'session expired' state is surfaced only by an opened setup modal, not a clear notice. FIX (sessionIdleReloginEase, UX-only): surface the reason clearly + document the knobs (Settings 'Store password on this device'; server SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECS) + optionally a longer default idle window. Severity Low (UX; no defect, no data effect). discoveredInField=true."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "sessionIdleReloginEase"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from pluginSyncRemovalPropagation to issuePluginSyncStaleServerState;
    #Resolves dependency from pluginSyncDisplayName to issuePluginSyncFolderIdNotName;
    #Resolves dependency from vaultChangeTransitionGuard to issueVaultChangeSkipsTransition;
    #Resolves dependency from sessionIdleReloginEase to issueSessionIdleReloginFriction;

    // ===== Field-reported 2026-07-30 (device log): the auth-retry storm. Resolved by the connection-FSM
    // typed-failure redesign (connectionFsmTypedFailure, 1.9.1). =====
    part issueAuthRetryStormNoStop : Issue { :>> id = "d7c1800d-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4008d"; :>> title = "A hard auth failure (rejected sign-in / 401) is retried forever like a network drop — hammering login into a server-side lockout, and mislabeled 'offline'"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-30"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported with a device log: after a token rejection at reconnect, the client looped 'connect FAILED: unauthorized' every ~15-30s for 45+ min, never prompting re-auth. Root cause: SyncEngine.failToOffline collapsed EVERY effect failure into ONE 'offline' state + ONE recovery (scheduleReconnect, backoff capped 30s), and doConnect's catch distinguished only 404/session-expired via lastIssue string REGEXES; a 401-login or a 429 fell through to retry-forever. Two compounding harms: (1) the storm can trip the server's own 10-fails/5-min LoginThrottle (throttle.rs) → a self-inflicted 429 lockout that persists (every attempt keeps the window from rolling); (2) isAuthError = /HTTP 401/ never matched the server's plain-text 'unauthorized' body (errText overwrites the HTTP-nnn string), so the reactive re-login-once ALSO never fired for a real status() 401. The status card mislabeled a rejected sign-in / a timeout as 'offline'. FIX (connectionFsmTypedFailure, 1.9.1): a typed-failure connection FSM — a separate LinkState machine + a pure classifier reading TAGGED error fields (never a message regex) + a recovery policy — that STOPS retrying on a terminal class (awaits the user + a slow self-heal re-probe), honors the 429 Retry-After window, and surfaces the specific reason; isAuthError fixed to read the status field. Guard (D0047): the connstate tests (classify table + strand/loop invariants). Severity High (self-inflicted lockout + can't sync + confusing state). discoveredInField=true (owner, real device log)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "connectionFsmTypedFailure"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    #Resolves dependency from connectionFsmTypedFailure to issueAuthRetryStormNoStop;

    // ===== Owner-requested 2026-07-31: a newly-synced plugin needs a restart to activate. Resolved by
    // hotLoadSyncedPlugins (1.11.0), scoped to a provably-private vault (owner-accepted security tradeoff). =====
    part issueSyncedPluginRestart : Issue { :>> id = "d7c1800e-0000-4a10-9b20-a1b2c3d4008e"; :>> title = "A community plugin synced to a device that didn't have it requires a full Obsidian restart to activate — deliberate for untrusted (shared) code, but friction on a single-user private vault"; :>> createdAt = "2026-07-31"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported: a plugin synced to a device (not previously there) shows 'not active yet — restart' and only loads on a full Obsidian restart. This is BY DESIGN (Round-6 SEC / R14 sec#1): sync-delivered plugin code is untrusted (a share peer / shared-vault owner could push malicious main.js), so SelfSync never auto-executes it — the restart is the trust barrier. But for a SINGLE-USER own+unshared vault the code is the user's OWN, so the restart is pure friction. FIX (hotLoadSyncedPlugins, 1.11.0): a NARROW, owner-ACCEPTED exception — on a PROVABLY-PRIVATE vault (own, not shared TO us, no readWrite grant/link — refreshVaultPrivacy, fail-safe) a newly-arrived plugin is hot-loaded live (app.plugins.loadManifests + enablePlugin), no restart. A shared/shareable vault, a running-plugin update, a hot-load failure, and CSS keep the restart barrier. The owner was shown the RCE tradeoff and chose the 'own, unshared only' scope. Severity Low (UX; the security-critical default — the restart barrier — is preserved for any untrusted case). discoveredInField=true (owner)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "hotLoadSyncedPlugins"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from hotLoadSyncedPlugins to issueSyncedPluginRestart;

    // ===== 2026-08-01 critique pass (4 independent antagonistic critics over shipped 1.9.0-1.11.0;
    // each finding re-verified against the code before recording). Three HIGH defects fixed in 1.11.1;
    // the Medium/Low findings backlogged. D0080: critique findings become severity-carrying Issues. =====
    part issueHotLoadPrivacyGateStale : Issue { :>> id = "f351b270-6d3a-4037-968e-904636df6bce"; :>> title = "Hot-load privacy gate (1.11.0) rides a STALE vaultIsPrivate — RCE-barrier bypass: a private->shared switch or an out-of-band grant lets a peer's synced main.js auto-execute with no restart"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-01"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Security critic finding, verified in code: vaultIsPrivate is never reset on a vault switch; flushConfigReload (which hot-loads) runs BEFORE the connect-time refreshVaultPrivacy (itself fire-and-forget void); and the delta/poll path that also hot-loads never refreshes it. So switching from a private vault to a shared/attacker vault, OR receiving an out-of-band readWrite grant on your own vault, leaves the gate true -> applyPluginCodeChange hot-loads a peer-controlled plugin (loadManifests+enablePlugin) with no restart — a bypass of the R14 sec#1 never-auto-execute barrier, within the in-scope peer/shared-vault threat model. FIX (hotLoadPrivacyGateFreshCheck, 1.11.1): re-derive privacy FRESH at the decision point. discoveredInField=false (critique-found)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "hotLoadPrivacyGateFreshCheck"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    #Resolves dependency from hotLoadPrivacyGateFreshCheck to issueHotLoadPrivacyGateStale;
    part issueReconcilePathTokenExpiryBlock : Issue { :>> id = "fdefcf4c-f6de-479a-befe-da2ef3fa9b39"; :>> title = "A routine mid-session token expiry on the poll/reconcile path mis-classifies as a PERMANENT auth block ('check your password') — the inverse of the auth-storm the FSM fixed"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-01"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "FSM critic finding, verified in code: doConnect re-logs-in once on a 401, but the reconcile effects (doReconcileAll/doReconcilePath) call this.api.* directly with no such retry. So when a token expires DURING a connected session, a poll-path 401 propagates straight to classifyConnectError -> (hasStoredPassword) AuthRejected -> blocked{authRejected} showing 'Sign-in rejected — check your password' for a VALID password, self-healing only via the 600s reprobe (which runs doConnect) — and PERMANENTLY on a WS-less mobile client until then. FIX (reconcilePathReloginOnce, 1.11.1): withSyncRelogin wraps the reconcile effects for one silent re-login + retry when a password is stored. discoveredInField=false (critique-found)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "reconcilePathReloginOnce"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    #Resolves dependency from reconcilePathReloginOnce to issueReconcilePathTokenExpiryBlock;
    part issueVaultKeyServerBlind : Issue { :>> id = "16e029b6-2117-46a7-8ce8-77c2003b72ef"; :>> title = "Vault-identity key is SERVER-BLIND (owner/vault) — repointing at a different server with the same vault name silently overwrites local files (D0047 guard never trips)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-01"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Vault-change critic finding, verified in code: historyFloorKey() = `${vaultOwner}/${vaultId}` omits the server URL, and the setup wizard's vaultChanged compares vaultId name only. Two servers trivially both host owner=''/vault='notes'. Repoint this device (own vault, same name) at a different server B and the D0047 guard's baseVaultKey === historyFloorKey() stays equal -> a plain reconcileFull runs against server B against a FOREIGN base -> for every path where B===L but R differs, decide() returns pull -> local file silently overwritten, no conflict copy. FIX (vaultKeyServerQualified, 1.11.1): server-qualified `host|owner/vault` key in both the guard + the wizard, with old-key grandfathering. discoveredInField=false (critique-found)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "vaultKeyServerQualified"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    #Resolves dependency from vaultKeyServerQualified to issueVaultKeyServerBlind;
    // Backlog (recorded, NOT fixed here) — clustered per critic area; each resolves to a stub action.
    part issueFsmCritiqueBacklog : Issue { :>> id = "fefd34b2-0f02-4893-9290-824935392946"; :>> title = "Connection-FSM Medium/Low follow-ups: LinkState not reset on disconnect (stale vault-gone prompt), serverDegraded reindex message clobbered, MFA/must-change-pw body-string classification, dead UserRetry edge / 429 re-arm / 503 retry-after"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-01"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "FSM critic Medium/Low findings (verified), backlogged behind the 3 HIGH fixes: F2 disconnect/unload leave LinkState so getLastIssue/isVaultGone leak a stale blocked/vault-gone prompt after disconnect; F3 the actionable serverDegraded 'run reindex' message is overwritten by the generic outer catch and shows 'Reconnecting…'; F4 MFA/must-change-password classification matches un-pinned server body strings; F5 the UserRetry LinkState edge is dead, a repeated 429 re-arms retryAfter each cycle, and Retry-After is honored only for 429. discoveredInField=false (critique-found)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "critiqueBacklogFsm"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from critiqueBacklogFsm to issueFsmCritiqueBacklog;
    part issueTimestampGateCritiqueBacklog : Issue { :>> id = "b23db4c3-7413-4e72-8b75-46b8247d384d"; :>> title = "Timestamp content-identity Medium/Low follow-ups: Date.parse value-shape gate over-masks bare-year/manual dates (contradicts the UI promise) + non-portable V8/JSCore; trailing-newline inconsistency; normHash never written"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-01"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Timestamp critic Medium/Low findings (verified), on the DEFAULT-ON feature so wide blast radius. T1 the isTimestampValue gate uses Date.parse, so a bare-year `created: 2020` (or a manually-curated date) is masked -> editing it silently doesn't propagate, contradicting the settings UI's 'a non-date value under the same key still syncs'. T2 Date.parse is engine-defined -> a non-canonical value hashes differently on desktop V8 vs mobile JSCore -> cross-platform churn. T4 trailing-newline normalization differs between normalizedContent (collapse 2+->1) and merge3's sameIgnoringEol (strip all) -> a final-newline toggle churns; lone-CR not normalized. T3 normHash is declared/serialized/read but never written (override rides on base.text recompute; >1MiB gap documented-accepted). discoveredInField=false (critique-found)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "critiqueBacklogTimestamp"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from critiqueBacklogTimestamp to issueTimestampGateCritiqueBacklog;
    part issueVaultTransitionModalBacklog : Issue { :>> id = "862badb3-41bf-469f-82a1-515e65b2233f"; :>> title = "Vault-change Medium/Low follow-ups: redeem + owner-axis + guard-driven changes bypass the resolution modal (silent merge = cross-vault union); empty-base guard skip; fire-and-forget key stamp"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-01"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Vault-change critic Medium/Low findings (verified): V2 setupwizard finishRedeem + the direct-adopt path reconnect() with NO resolution modal (only the vaultId-name axis was closed), and the guard's remedy is a SILENT merge (a union across two vaults = contamination) rather than the Download/Upload/Cancel adjudication SwitchVaultModal offers — route guard-detected changes through the same modal. V3 the guard requires base.paths().length so a vault change with an empty base but local files skips it (pushes local into the new vault); baseVaultKey is stamped via a fire-and-forget save. discoveredInField=false (critique-found)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "critiqueBacklogVault"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from critiqueBacklogVault to issueVaultTransitionModalBacklog;
    part issueSecurityHardeningBacklog : Issue { :>> id = "728d92a6-5e0e-4b65-bd6f-b1ed7bec05f6"; :>> title = "Security Medium/Low follow-ups: hot-load privacy gate trusts SERVER-reported share metadata (malicious-server threat — needs an owner decision); removePluginFromServer id not validated + re-push re-hot-loads"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-01"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Security critic Medium/Low findings (verified): S2 (threat-model decision) vaultIsPrivate is derived from share metadata the SERVER reports, and that same server ships the plugin bytes — a compromised/hostile server could lie 'not shared' -> hot-load. In scope only if the self-hosted server is treated as untrusted; needs an owner decision (accept-risk vs client-side attestation vs revert the private-vault hot-load exception). S3 (LOW) removePluginFromServer(id) builds a delete prefix from a server/peer-influenced id with no validation (reject `..`/`/`/empty); a removed plugin re-pushed by another active device re-arrives and, on a private vault, hot-loads again without re-approval. discoveredInField=false (critique-found)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "critiqueBacklogSecurity"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from critiqueBacklogSecurity to issueSecurityHardeningBacklog;

    // Discovered 2026-08-02 while dispositioning the FSM backlog (F4): a FEATURE gap, not a critique defect.
    part issueMfaLoginUnsupported : Issue { :>> id = "5a8c473e-6c0d-440c-82e5-3607ec81d2b7"; :>> title = "MFA-enrolled accounts can't sign in via the plugin — login sends no TOTP code and the wizard/reconfigure has no field for one"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "The server fully implements TOTP MFA (enroll/confirm, and login returns 401 'mfa required' without a code), but the plugin's transport.login() posts only {username,password} and the setup/reconfigure UI collects no code — so a user who enrolls MFA is locked out of the plugin. The connection FSM correctly classifies it (MfaRequired -> 'enter your authenticator code (Reconfigure)'), but Reconfigure can't actually collect the code, so the message is a dead-end. Surfaced while confirming the F4 classify branch matches the server's real phrasing. This is a FEATURE gap (MFA login support), not a defect in the shipped 1.9.1 classifier. discoveredInField=false (found during the critique-backlog pass)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "mfaLoginSupport"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    // D0038: MFA deprecated instead of building plugin TOTP — the mooting Decision now resolves this
    // (was: #Resolves from mfaLoginSupport, the not-pursued add-plugin-TOTP action, now removed).
    #Resolves dependency from d0038 to issueMfaLoginUnsupported;

    // Field-reported 2026-08-02 (owner, mobile, on 1.11.2).
    part issueStatusSwitchingLatched : Issue { :>> id = "6009ef6a-83ca-4211-9e6b-a50693e11330"; :>> title = "Status card latches 'Switching vault… applying your choice' on a persisted pendingSwitch even while connected + synced (no switch initiated); an authoritative switch that lingered set also re-clobbers local edits every connect"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported on a real device: the mobile status card showed 'Switching vault… applying your choice' persistently while the vault was actually connected and synced (green dot, recent Last synced), with no switch initiated. Root cause: statusDisplay() short-circuited on settings.pendingSwitch (a PERSISTED config value = the switch resolution) and this.resuming (a loose bool) BEFORE consulting the pure Phase projection — so a pendingSwitch that lingered set (its switchTo reconcile killed mid-flight on mobile before it could clear) latched the label. The D0047 guard was confirmed NOT the trigger (no 'base belonged to' line in the owner's debug log). Second-order (critique HIGH): a lingering AUTHORITATIVE resolution (download=take-remote / upload=take-local) re-runs on every connect and re-clobbers local edits made since. FIX (statusDisplayPureProjection, 1.11.3): statusDisplay is a PURE Phase projection (no setting/flag read); an already-applied guard clears a lingering switch whose base already belongs to the target (no re-clobber); the settings-card dot is made realtime-aware (no divergence). discoveredInField=true (owner, mobile)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "statusDisplayPureProjection"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    #Resolves dependency from statusDisplayPureProjection to issueStatusSwitchingLatched;

    // Field-reported 2026-08-02 (owner, mobile) — surfaced by the 1.11.3 debug log.
    part issueSwitchOutageFalseSynced : Issue { :>> id = "53288824-2bc7-4abc-8b85-1bd9318858ec"; :>> title = "A vault switch (upload/download) swallows a whole-server outage (DNS unresolvable) as per-file skips and settles to a FALSE 'Synced (polling)' — the status lies when the server is unreachable"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner's 1.11.3 debug log on mobile: a persisted 'upload' switch ran during a DNS outage (Unable to resolve host 'notes2.willweatherholtz.com') and produced a flood of per-file 'UnknownHostException … skipped it, other files continue', after which the status card showed 'Synced (polling)' (yellow) even though the server was completely unreachable. Root cause: the normal reconcile loops (reconcileAll/reconcileLocalConfig) abort a pass on isConnectionError (→ engine offline+backoff), but switchTo's helpers switchDownload/switchUpload did NOT — every server-op catch called onFileError (skip+continue). So a connection-wide outage was isolated per-file, the switch 'completed' with everything skipped, and the engine settled to idle → a false 'synced'. FIX (switchAbortOnOutage, 1.11.4): switchDownload (applyPull) + switchUpload (pushFile, deleteFile) re-throw on isConnectionError, mirroring reconcileAll → the switch aborts → 'Reconnecting…' (honest). The underlying host-unresolvable condition is the owner's network/server to diagnose. discoveredInField=true (owner, mobile)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "switchAbortOnOutage"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    #Resolves dependency from switchAbortOnOutage to issueSwitchOutageFalseSynced;

    // Field-reported 2026-08-02 (owner, mobile) — the deeper layer surfaced by the 1.11.4 log.
    part issueConnectFalseSyncedDuringInitialReconcile : Issue { :>> id = "637d7422-517b-4012-b9b7-5387a0ed6732"; :>> title = "During a failing/empty initial connect reconcile the card reads 'Synced (polling)' — markReconciling pre-flips to 'syncing', which effectivePhase collapses to idle when 0 files transfer"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "After switchAbortOnOutage (1.11.4) the switch correctly aborted on the DNS outage (connect FAILED -> retrying in 3s), but the owner's log/UI still showed 'Synced (polling)' during the failing ~30s connect attempts — 'nothing to indicate work is being done'. Root cause (one FSM layer below the pure-projection fix): markReconciling() optimistically flipped connecting/disconnected -> reconciling (phase 'syncing') the instant the health check passed, before the initial reconcile ran; when that reconcile transferred nothing (DNS failing), syncPending stayed 0 and effectivePhase collapsed syncing-0-pending -> idle -> a FALSE 'Synced (polling)'. A normal poll avoided this by never pre-flipping (beginReconcile escalates only on real work). FIX (connectPhaseNoOptimisticFlip, 1.11.5): removed markReconciling; beginReconcile is the single door into 'reconciling', escalating from idle OR an in-flight connect, and onProgress(pending>0) triggers it — so a failing/empty connect stays 'Connecting…'/'Reconnecting…'. discoveredInField=true (owner, mobile)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "connectPhaseNoOptimisticFlip"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    #Resolves dependency from connectPhaseNoOptimisticFlip to issueConnectFalseSyncedDuringInitialReconcile;

    // Field-reported 2026-08-02 (owner, mobile) — surfaced by the 1.11.5 log.
    part issueSwitchNoProgressLooksHung : Issue { :>> id = "c2405aec-9b9f-463e-9ca5-b52d428eaba0"; :>> title = "A vault switch (upload/download) reports no progress, so it sits on a static 'Connecting…' and looks hung during a long transfer"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "After connectPhaseNoOptimisticFlip (1.11.5) the card honestly showed 'Connecting…' during the initial reconcile, but the owner reported it looked 'hung on connecting?' during a persisted 'upload' switch. Root cause: the normal reconcile (reconcileAll) reports onProgress (a decrementing pending count), but switchTo/switchUpload/switchDownload reported NONE — so syncPending stayed 0, there was no 'Syncing… N' detail and no state escalation, and a long/large switch sat on a static 'Connecting…'. FIX (switchReportsProgress, 1.11.6): switchDownload + switchUpload report onProgress like reconcileAll (full count up front, decrement to 0) — so an active switch shows 'Syncing… N pending' decreasing, a stalled one a stuck count, and an outage still aborts to 'Reconnecting…'. discoveredInField=true (owner, mobile). NOTE: the underlying block remains that notes2.willweatherholtz.com is unreachable (the owner's server/DNS)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "switchReportsProgress"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from switchReportsProgress to issueSwitchNoProgressLooksHung;

    // Field-reported 2026-08-02 (owner, mobile) — 'is it rechecking? I don't see it reattempt'.
    part issueMobileResumeNoReattempt : Issue { :>> id = "c720d44e-f0d2-4ffc-9eca-2ac1352c1972"; :>> title = "On mobile, a connection that failed while backgrounded does not re-attempt on resume — the backoff timer is suspended and onResume's remote event is dropped while disconnected"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner (mobile), server URL confirmed reachable: the plugin appeared to stop rechecking after a failed connect — 'is it rechecking though? I don't see it reattempt.' Root cause (mobile context-switching): the backoff reconnect timer uses window.setTimeout, which the OS SUSPENDS while the app is backgrounded, so a pending retry sits paused; and onResume enqueued a `remote` event, which the engine DROPS while disconnected (work is ignored until connected). So foregrounding after a failed connect did nothing to re-attempt. FIX (resumeReattemptsConnection, 1.11.7): onResume, when disconnected, cancels the stale paused timer and enqueues a fresh `connect` immediately (+ logs 'app resumed — re-checking the connection' for visibility); connected → re-assess; 'off' left alone. discoveredInField=true (owner, mobile)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "resumeReattemptsConnection"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    #Resolves dependency from resumeReattemptsConnection to issueMobileResumeNoReattempt;

    // Found 2026-08-02 by an STPA-UCA hunt on the delete/overwrite control actions (NOT the field) — the
    // first bug the deepened analysis DISCOVERED rather than confirmed.
    part issueSwitchUploadFalseAbsenceDelete : Issue { :>> id = "f6e60146-38f9-42f2-8b5c-03efe55e8c11"; :>> title = "An upload vault-switch tombstones a remote file that io.list() transiently FALSELY omits — fleet-wide delete-local of a still-present file (L-1)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "STPA-UCA hunt finding (delete-remote guideword): switchUpload's remote-drop loop issued delete-remote — a server tombstone that propagates delete-local to every peer — for any path missing from the switch's SINGLE io.list() snapshot, with NO probePresence re-probe and NO bulk-ratio guard. io.list() is fallible (an un-hydrated OneDrive/cloud placeholder, a directory that fails to enumerate, an OS/AV lock transiently under-reports a still-present file — the exact issueFalseAbsenceDelete hazard). On an 'upload' switch (make this vault authoritative) such a file is then never pushed AND deleteFile'd on the server; peers see the tombstone and remove their genuine local copy (L-1 data loss), while this device keeps the bytes with no base entry until the next full scan. This was the ONE delete path that trusted the raw listing: reconcileOne + reconcileDelta re-probe via probePresence and switchDownload's local-mirror carries the bulk-ratio guard — switchUpload had neither. FIX (switchUploadProbeGuard, 1.11.8): re-probe (probePresence) before tombstoning; present/indeterminate ⇒ keep. Severity High (fleet-wide delete of real content; recovery timing/luck-dependent via .trash + next full scan). discoveredInField=false (STPA-hunt-found)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "switchUploadProbeGuard"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    #Resolves dependency from switchUploadProbeGuard to issueSwitchUploadFalseAbsenceDelete;
    // Found 2026-08-02 by the same hunt (config-apply guideword) — a Medium L-3 gap needing an owner decision.
    part issueUntrustedCssApplied : Issue { :>> id = "90c02a08-7032-4b43-9c44-e57f413b8fd9"; :>> title = "Untrusted CSS (theme/snippet) synced from a shared vault is applied with only a restart notice — no trust warning, no private-vault gate, unlike plugin code (L-3)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "STPA-UCA hunt finding (caApplyConfig, L-3, Medium): flushConfigReload classifies synced themes/snippets/appearance CSS as 'will apply after restart' (a bare Notice) on the premise that CSS is 'never auto-applied' — but the plugin WRITES the CSS to disk during reconcile, and Obsidian hot-reloads an ENABLED snippet/theme on file change without a restart. On a readWrite-shared vault (snippets default ON) a peer can overwrite an enabled snippet's .css and Obsidian applies it live; CSS in Obsidian's un-CSP'd renderer enables exfil (background:url(...)) + phishing overlays (a class the code's own comment acknowledges). UCA-7 NAMES CSS but its realized mitigation SR-5 gates only plugin CODE (the restart trust-barrier + private-vault hot-load); CSS gets neither the trust warning nor the private-vault gate. Partly accepted-by-parity with Obsidian Sync (which also applies synced snippets) — so this NEEDS AN OWNER DECISION (gate/trust-warn untrusted shared-vault CSS vs accept-by-parity), not a unilateral fix. discoveredInField=false (STPA-hunt-found). Separately, the hunt re-confirmed the accepted S2 malicious-server oracle risk (issueSecurityHardeningBacklog) and sharpened it (the codebase elsewhere defends against a compromised server) — noted for the owner's re-weighing, already accept-risk'd."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "cssTrustGate"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from cssTrustGate to issueUntrustedCssApplied;

    // Found 2026-08-02 by an STPA-UCA hunt on the SERVER's delete/overwrite control actions (not the field).
    // The owner chose the CAS-guard fix over accept-authoritative via an explicit AskUserQuestion.
    part issueDeleteNoCasLostUpdate : Issue { :>> id = "3d5f9a71-8c02-4e6b-b1a4-0f2c7d9e4a83"; :>> title = "DELETE has no optimistic-concurrency (CAS) guard, unlike COMMIT — a stale reconcile delete silently supersedes a newer concurrent commit (L-1 lost update)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "STPA-UCA hunt finding (server delete control action, L-1). Vault::commit CAS-guards optimistic concurrency (expected_version: a second committer based on a stale version 409s + re-reconciles + merges), with a code comment naming the lost-update it prevents. Vault::delete had NO such guard: it took only a path and computed new_version unconditionally. So a device holding a STALE base could delete a file another device just edited (A commits edit v5->v6; B, still at v5, deletes -> server tombstones at v7), and the editor pulls the tombstone and silently loses its change FLEET-WIDE with no conflict marker — the exact class commit's CAS exists to stop. Weaponizable: a readWrite grantee can DELETE to discard the owner's concurrent edit. The critic flagged it as a possible 'delete-authoritative' trade-off that was NOWHERE recorded as a Decision, with the CAS rationale arguing against it — 'warrants an explicit decision.' OWNER DECISION (AskUserQuestion, 2026-08-02): CAS-guard delete (symmetric with commit) over accept-authoritative. FIX (deleteRemoteCasGuard): Vault::delete_checked(path, expected_version) mirrors commit's CAS (AlreadyExists->409); the reconcile delete-remote sends the based-on remote version; deliberate/authoritative deletes (user gesture / adjudication / switch) omit it and still always win. On a 409 the client re-reconciles and, because decide() keys on hashes, remote-advanced-since-base reads as edit-wins-pull -> the edit is RESURRECTED, never lost. Backward-compatible both ways (old client omits the field; old server ignores the unknown query param). discoveredInField=false (STPA-hunt-found)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "deleteRemoteCasGuard"; :>> severity = Severity::High; }
    #Resolves dependency from deleteRemoteCasGuard to issueDeleteNoCasLostUpdate;
    // Secondary LOW from the same server hunt — a mirror mismatch (not data loss); the chunk store stays authoritative.
    part issueMirrorWriteNotSelfHealed : Issue { :>> id = "7b1e0c4a-5d93-4f27-9a86-2e3c8b0d61f5"; :>> title = "A failed bind-mount mirror write is not repaired by a later idempotent commit retry (the same-content short-circuit returns before write_mirror)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "STPA-UCA server hunt, LOW. Vault::commit writes the authoritative bytes to the chunk store (durable) and then BEST-EFFORT mirrors them to the bind-mount file; a mirror-write failure is logged, not fatal, because get_chunk serves the durable content. But the idempotent re-commit short-circuit (identical path+hash+chunks -> return unchanged, no persist) returns BEFORE write_mirror, so a commit whose mirror write failed is NOT healed by a subsequent identical retry — the on-disk mirror stays stale until the next genuine content change or a reindex. NOT data loss (the chunk store is authoritative + is what sync serves); only affects a user editing the vault DIRECTLY on the host filesystem via the bind mount (a niche mode). Fix would re-attempt the mirror write on the idempotent path when the mirror is absent/stale. discoveredInField=false (STPA-hunt-found)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "mirrorWriteSelfHealBacklog"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from mirrorWriteSelfHealBacklog to issueMirrorWriteNotSelfHealed;

    // Raised 2026-08-02 by the owner ('does diagnose actually do much? always says everything is fine even
    // if it's not working'). An L-5 (misleading-UX) gap; the owner directed removal over a heavier fix.
    part issueDiagnoseFalseAllClear : Issue { :>> id = "2f8c1d64-9b3a-4e07-a5f2-6d09e83b71c4"; :>> title = "The 'Diagnose' button gives a FALSE 'all good' — it probes only the connection chain, blind to whether sync is actually converging (L-5 misleading UX)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported. HttpTransport.diagnose walked the CONNECTION chain (reachable -> version -> auth -> vault present -> server-index-not-degraded) via fresh out-of-band HTTP probes and returned 'All good' when all five links were healthy. But it NEVER consulted the live sync engine (getState/linkState/getPhase/pending, per-file retry budget), so it was structurally blind to sync NON-convergence: a stalled/off engine, pending work not draining, a file looping on an error, or a wedged FSM. So exactly when sync is stuck-but-the-pipe-is-fine — the case the user clicks Diagnose for — it falsely says 'everything is fine' (an L-5 misleading feedback, feeding a wrong picture into the USER's process model; relates to H-9 reported!=actual). Worse: it was a SECOND, out-of-band source of truth that could CONTRADICT the honest status card (a pure FSM projection) built this sitting — the dual-source anti-pattern the status-FSM work exists to kill. Analysis showed the honest status card already surfaces every actionable cause diagnose gave, via connstate.blockedTip (version mismatch / sign-in rejected / session expired / MFA / vault-gone / access-removed / degraded) derived from REAL sync failures. RESOLUTION (owner-directed 'consider just removing'): REMOVE the Diagnose button + its chain (removeDiagnoseButton, 1.11.11) so the honest FSM-projected status line is the SINGLE source of truth (it cannot give a false all-clear because it is a pure projection). discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Medium (misleading, not data loss)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "removeDiagnoseButton"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from removeDiagnoseButton to issueDiagnoseFalseAllClear;

    // Owner-reported 2026-08-02: 'hanging on connecting status. I'd like some insight into why - surely
    // connecting has multiple phases?' An L-5 (missing/inadequate-feedback) gap on the connect loop.
    part issueConnectStatusOpaque : Issue { :>> id = "6b3e9f21-4c7a-4d80-b2e5-1f8a0c6d9e34"; :>> title = "'Connecting…' is one opaque label over several phases with no sub-phase or timing — a slow phase reads as hung with zero insight (L-5)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported (field): the status hung on 'connecting' with no insight into why; the log showed 'connecting to ... as will' then a 36s gap then 'config sync on — syncing 38 files' and nothing between. doConnect (main.ts) runs THREE phases all labeled 'Connecting…' and logs NOTHING between them on the happy path: (1) establishSession — acquireToken (login/cached) -> api.status() first authed round-trip (30s timeout) -> version handshake -> refreshShareGrant; (2) resolveAndApplySwitch -> reconcileFull -> reconcileAll — which fetches the FULL remote manifest (changes(0)) THEN lists+hashes every local file (the 'config sync on' log is emitted by io.list, so the 36s of silence was establishSession + the manifest fetch, BEFORE the local scan even started); (3) finishConnect — WS + poll. By design (connectPhaseNoOptimisticFlip 1.11.5) the status stays 'Connecting…' through the whole initial reconcile and only flips to 'Syncing…' on real transfer work — good against a false 'Synced', but it makes a slow initial scan/fetch look hung. The user (a controller) can't tell a slow connect from a stuck one and has no timing to locate the stall — an L-5 inadequate-feedback gap on the connect control loop (parallels S-14, the no-progress bulk op). RESOLUTION (owner directed 'Status sub-phases + timed log'): connectPhaseVisibility (1.12.0) names each sub-phase (signing in / checking the server / checking your access / fetching changes / scanning local files / reconciling N) as the 'Connecting…' DETAIL (card/tooltip) AND logs each with cumulative elapsed, driven by a new reconcile onStage hook + a connect-gated setConnectStage. discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Medium (misleading/opaque, not data loss)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "connectPhaseVisibility"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from connectPhaseVisibility to issueConnectStatusOpaque;

    // Found 2026-08-02 by the client-side chunking/reassembly STPA-UCA hunt (an independent critic). The
    // owner chose to fix it (AskUserQuestion 'Fix it — careful spec + critique + TDD'). Closes SR-2's
    // previously-accepted streamed-pull residual.
    part issueStreamedPullMidEditLoss : Issue { :>> id = "8d4f1a63-2c90-4e57-b6a1-3f0e7c85d924"; :>> title = "A mid-stream local edit is silently, unrecoverably lost on a large-file (>=8 MiB) streamed pull — the buffered path is safe, the streamed path was not (L-1)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Client chunking/reassembly STPA-UCA hunt finding (UCA-4 / S-3 / SR-2 family). applyPull's streamed branch (rmeta.size >= STREAM_MIN_BYTES, 8 MiB) ran the racing-edit check (conflictCopyIfRaced) BEFORE streaming, then streamFileToDisk reassembled to a temp file and atomic-renamed it over `path` in close() with NO re-check in the seam. So an edit saved to that file DURING the multi-second download was clobbered by the rename, and base was set to the remote hash → next reconcile sees local==base → in-sync → the edit is gone, unrecoverably. The BUFFERED path (all files < 8 MiB) is SAFE — it re-checks AFTER the fetch (reconcile.ts). Asymmetry: small notes protected, large files (e.g. a live-edited >8 MiB .canvas) not. This was EXACTLY SR-2's documented 'Residual (accepted): a sub-window TOCTOU on very large streamed pulls.' Prior attempts to close it regressed (a 'post-write re-read' that read the file AFTER the rename = remote bytes, laundering wrong bytes into base) — so the fix needed care. FIX (streamedPullRacingEditGuard 1.12.1): streamFileToDisk gains a beforeRename hook called after the incremental hash-verify + before close()'s rename; applyPull passes a seam guard that reuses conflictCopyIfRaced on the ORIGINAL on-disk file (which still holds the racing edit — never the post-rename result, so base is only ever rmeta.hash). An independent critic (SOUND-WITH-CHANGES) caught a follow-on hole: the orphan-cleanup would delete the SOLE surviving racing edit if close() throws AFTER the rename applied — so the fix does NOT delete the seam copy on failure (a redundant copy is cosmetic; a deleted edit is loss), plus a close()-must-never-throw-after-rename contract. Guards (fail-first): reconcile.test 'preserves a mid-stream local edit as a conflict copy', 'makes NO spurious copy when there is no racing edit', 'a >8 MiB config file ... ABORTS (adjudicate)', 'a close() failure AFTER the rename does NOT delete the sole surviving racing edit'. discoveredInField=false (STPA-hunt-found). Severity Medium (narrow window — >=8 MiB files hand-edited mid-download; unrecoverable but rare; notes unaffected)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "streamedPullRacingEditGuard"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from streamedPullRacingEditGuard to issueStreamedPullMidEditLoss;

    // Owner-reported 2026-08-02 (field, via the new connect-phase timing): 'second time it's reconciling
    // ~1100 files, fully synced last time, changed nothing.' A reload-rehash-the-whole-vault perf issue.
    part issueScanSkipHintNotPersisted : Issue { :>> id = "9e5a2c74-3d81-4f60-b7a2-4c0f8d96e135"; :>> title = "Every plugin reload re-reads + re-hashes the WHOLE vault on the first connect — the size/mtime scan-skip hint was deliberately not persisted"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported via the 1.12.0 connect timing: on a fresh 'plugin loaded' the first connect reconciles ~1137 files though nothing changed and it was fully synced. Root cause: the (size,mtime) scan-skip hint (which lets a whole-vault reconcile skip the read+SHA-256 of an unchanged file) was DELIBERATELY in-memory-only — BaseStore.toJSON (base.ts) persisted only hash/text/normHash, dropping size/mtime ('never let a perf hint outlive its verifying session', R15 sync#3). So after every reload the base has no stamps, baseStatUnchanged can't match, and reconcileOne re-reads+re-hashes every file (nothing transfers — still fully synced — but it re-does the verification, the field cost). Also surfaced by the same log: the ~18s pre-reconcile is io.list() walking the whole .obsidian config tree (SEPARATE issue, not this one), and 'token rejected — re-logging in' every connect (session token not reused; separate). OWNER DECISION (AskUserQuestion 'Persist the scan-skip hints'): persist size+mtime in toJSON so a reload trusts the same rsync/Syncthing (size,mtime) heuristic the in-session skip already uses. TRADEOFF (independent critic verified SOUND — no data-loss path): a file changed OUT of Obsidian while off, preserving BOTH size AND mtime, isn't re-hashed on reload — the exact accepted rsync residual, now WIDER (the reload full-rehash backstop is removed, so the rare miss self-heals only on the next edit event / size|mtime change / remote divergence, not on the next restart). It is a DELAYED push, NEVER data loss (a lying stamp colliding with a remote change re-reads live in applyPull->conflictCopyIfRaced and writes a conflict copy — never a silent overwrite), and never hides a REMOTE change (detected via the manifest). FIX (persistScanSkipHint 1.12.2): toJSON emits size+mtime (constructor already loads them); guarded by base.test (round-trip) + reconcile T2/T5 (unchanged skips read + noop), T3 (stale stamp still re-reads a change), T4 (lying stamp + remote divergence -> conflict copy, the safety test), T6 (absent file not masked -> delete-remote fires), and a main.ts mtime-source-parity invariant (T7). discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Medium (perf/UX; unrecoverable-loss ruled out by the critic)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "persistScanSkipHint"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from persistScanSkipHint to issueScanSkipHintNotPersisted;

    // Owner-reported 2026-08-02 (field, via 1.12.0 connect log): 'token rejected — re-logging in' on
    // connect. Owner directed 'fix the token one first'. A too-aggressive idle-timeout DEFAULT.
    part issueTokenIdleReloginFriction : Issue { :>> id = "a7c4e582-6d13-4b90-9f27-3e0a8c145d76"; :>> title = "The plugin re-logs-in on every connect after a break — the server's session idle-timeout defaulted to 30 min"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported via the 1.12.0 connect timing ('token rejected — re-logging in' -> 'login OK', ~0.5s, on connect). Cause: the server's bearer tokens (tokens.rs, persisted to .tokens.json so they survive a RESTART — not the cause) carry BOTH a 30-day absolute cap AND an IDLE timeout (AC.3.1.11), which DEFAULTED to 30 min (SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECS). An actively-syncing device slides last_used and never idle-expires, but a device whose app was CLOSED / not syncing for >30 min has its token idle-expire, so the next open 401s and the client (correctly) re-logs-in. For a personal single-user self-host where the client already stores the credential + auto-relogins, a 30-min idle timeout adds friction with little real security benefit (the re-login is automatic). OWNER DECISION (AskUserQuestion 'Default to the 30-day absolute cap (no idle expiry)'): make idle expiry DEFAULT-OFF. FIX (tokenIdleDefaultOff 1.12.3, SERVER — requires a server-image redeploy): DEFAULT_IDLE_TTL_SECS 30*60 -> 0 (idle expiry off; token lives its full 30-day absolute cap + revoke/password-change). The control REMAINS available: a compliance-seeking operator opts in via SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECS=<positive>. COMPLIANCE reconciled honestly (not silently): NIST 800-171 3.1.11 disposition partial -> operator (opt-in), and 3.13.9's inactivity clause noted opt-in; docs/cmmc-posture.md + docs/deployment.md doc-synced. Guards: tokens.rs default_has_no_idle_expiry_only_the_absolute_cap (new default) + idle_expires_a_stale_token_but_keeps_an_active_one (the control when enabled). discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Low (friction; auto-heals, no data/security loss). NOTE: two OTHER things from the same log remain separate — the ~18s .obsidian config-tree walk (owner directed 'then the other' — NEXT) and nothing else."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "tokenIdleDefaultOff"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from tokenIdleDefaultOff to issueTokenIdleReloginFriction;

    // Owner-reported 2026-08-02 (field, via 1.12.0 connect timing): the ~18s pre-reconcile 'scanning local
    // files' phase. Owner directed 'then the other' (after the token fix). A slow config-tree walk.
    part issueConfigWalkSlow : Issue { :>> id = "b8d5f296-4e02-4c71-a3d6-5f1a9e074c28"; :>> title = "The .obsidian config-tree enumeration is fully sequential — a plugin-heavy tree costs many seconds on a latency-bound (mobile) adapter, every reconcile"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported via the 1.12.0 connect timing: the 'scanning local files' phase took ~18s (2.5s -> 20.5s in the log), before the reconcile even started. Root cause: ObsidianVaultIo.enumerateConfig walked the hidden .obsidian/ tree FULLY SEQUENTIALLY — one awaited adapter.list(dir) per directory + one awaited adapter.stat(file) per passing file, depth-first — so the wall-clock was (call COUNT x per-call latency). The notes come from Obsidian's in-memory getFiles() instantly; only this config walk does async fs work, and it runs on EVERY reconcile (incl. polls), not just connect. On mobile (Android — the owner's device) each adapter call round-trips a filesystem bridge, so a plugin-heavy .obsidian tree (many plugin dirs/files) = seconds. OWNER DIRECTED the fix ('then the other'). FIX (configWalkParallel 1.12.4): extract the walk into a pure, unit-tested configwalk.ts (walkConfigTree) that runs adapter.list/stat CONCURRENTLY under a GLOBAL concurrency cap (a counting semaphore — a per-call mapPool can't bound a recursive walk), collapsing the wall-clock toward (tree DEPTH x latency). Read-only + behavior-preserving: it finds exactly the same passing-file set (order-independent), a dir it can't list is SKIPPED (not treated as empty — reconcile's per-file probe still guards a false delete) + logged, and a slow walk logs its shape ('config scan: N folders + M files in Xs') so the effect is measurable. Guards: configwalk.test — finds every passing file across a nested tree; respects the passes filter; a list error skips only that subtree + continues; NEVER exceeds the global cap (peak<=limit) yet DID run in parallel (peak>1); empty tree. discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Medium (perf; every-reconcile cost, worse on mobile)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "configWalkParallel"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from configWalkParallel to issueConfigWalkSlow;

    // Owner-reported 2026-08-02 (field): 'says it's reconciling but it's not actually doing anything?' then
    // 'stuck on label' — the connect status showed a static 'reconciling 1137 local file(s)' that both
    // overstated (implied syncing 1137) and, when the examination was slow, looked frozen. An L-5 gap.
    part issueReconcileExamOpaque : Issue { :>> id = "c9e6f307-5f13-4d82-b4e7-6a2b0f185d39"; :>> title = "The connect examination shows a static 'reconciling N files' — overstates (implies syncing) AND looks stuck when the pass is slow, with no progress"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported (field): the connect status showed 'reconciling 1137 local file(s)' and appeared to do nothing ('says reconciling but not doing anything'), then 'stuck on label'. Two problems: (1) LABEL — my connect sub-phase (1.12.0) said 'reconciling N local file(s)', which reads as 'syncing N' when the pass actually EXAMINES all files to detect changes and transfers only what CHANGED (nothing changed -> 0 work -> completes to Fully synced). (2) NO PROGRESS — the examination showed a STATIC label, so a SLOW full pass (a first-run re-hash before the 1.12.2 scan-skip stamps have persisted; or a large mobile vault) looks frozen, and a genuine hang is indistinguishable from slow. The engine can't flip to 'Fully synced' until reconcileAll returns, so a slow/hung pass leaves the connect on the stale label. FIX (reconcileExamProgress 1.12.5): (a) honest label 'checking N files for changes' (examines, not syncs; real transfer work still shows as 'Syncing… N pending' via onProgress); (b) LIVE progress 'checked X/N files' (every 250 examined) so a slow pass shows MOVEMENT (not fake-stuck) and a genuine hang is PINPOINTED — the count freezes at the stuck file. NOTE: this is a UX + DIAGNOSTIC fix; if the underlying stall is a hard hang (not a one-time slow re-hash that completes + persists stamps), the frozen progress count locates it for a targeted follow-up. Guards: reconcile.test onStage sequence updated (fetching -> scanning -> 'checking N files for changes'). discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Medium (misleading + looks-stuck; the connect blocks on the examination)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "reconcileExamProgress"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from reconcileExamProgress to issueReconcileExamOpaque;
    // Owner-directed 2026-08-02: 'get rid of the "remove from server" button ... admin interface ... taking
    // up far too much space for a rare need.'
    part issuePluginRemoveButtonClutter : Issue { :>> id = "d0f7a418-6024-4e73-a5f8-7b3c1a296e4a"; :>> title = "The per-plugin 'Remove from server' button clutters the plugin list — a whole button per row for a rare, admin-appropriate need"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-directed: the settings plugin list rendered a 'Remove from server' button on EVERY server-present plugin row (settings.ts renderPluginRows) — a whole button per row for a rare need, taking far too much space. Removing a plugin's files from the server is an owner/admin task better done from the admin interface. FIX (removePluginServerButton 1.12.5): remove the per-row button; the plugin's removePluginFromServer method (still tested — plugin-wiring purge + traversal-reject tests) stays for the admin/other path, just not surfaced as per-row UI clutter. No test asserted the button (tsc clean, DOM tests green). discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Low (UI declutter)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "removePluginServerButton"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from removePluginServerButton to issuePluginRemoveButtonClutter;

    // Found 2026-08-02 by the client-side WebSocket-push STPA-UCA hunt (independent critic). The only
    // non-null client finding; verified against the code.
    part issueWsSupersededOpenError : Issue { :>> id = "e1a80529-7135-4d94-b5f8-8c3d1b2a7e50"; :>> title = "The WS open/error handlers lack the superseded-socket identity guard that close has — a late error from an OLD socket degrades the CURRENT live socket"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Client WS-push STPA-UCA hunt finding (Medium). spinUpWs supersedes a socket by this.ws?.close() then pointing this.ws at the new one; the FSM design (transportstate.ts:7) requires ALL socket events to honor the this.ws!==ws identity check. The `close` handler and the liveness tick DO guard, but `open` (main.ts) and `error` did NOT. So a late `error` from a SUPERSEDED old socket (racing the abort supersession triggers) called applyTransport('errored') -> transportTransition(live,'errored') -> degraded -> the CURRENT live socket is forced to degraded: realtimeConnected reads false, the light shows yellow 'Synced (polling)' instead of green 'Fully synced', and the poll is pinned to POLL_ACTIVE_MS (4s) until the next full socket cycle (battery). SAFE direction (under-claims health; changes still flow via the poll -> no missed changes, no false-synced), so Medium not High; a clear bug (asymmetric guard contradicting the FSM's own supersession design). The dangerous direction (a stale `open` flipping to false-live) is unreachable (supersession close()s a still-connecting socket, which aborts without firing open) but the open guard is added for symmetry. FIX (wsSupersededGuard 1.12.6): add `if (this.unloading || !this.api || this.ws !== ws) return;` to both open + error handlers, matching close. Guard: plugin-wiring 'a SUPERSEDED WS socket's late open/error does NOT disturb the current live socket' (fail-first). discoveredInField=false (WS-hunt-found). Severity Medium (wrong status light + battery; no data risk)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "wsSupersededGuard"; :>> severity = Severity::Medium; }
    #Resolves dependency from wsSupersededGuard to issueWsSupersededOpenError;
    // Found 2026-08-02 by the SERVER-side WS-push hunt — a LOW latent-coupling / defense-in-depth gap (not
    // exploitable today). Backlogged (would need a server redeploy; not urgent).
    part issueWsSessionAliveMustChange : Issue { :>> id = "f2b91630-8246-4a05-c6a9-9d4e2c3b8f61"; :>> title = "The WS per-delivery liveness re-check (session_alive) omits the must_change dimension the connect-time gate enforces"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-02"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Server WS-push STPA-UCA hunt finding (Low, latent / defense-in-depth). The WS UPGRADE mirrors the HTTP must_change gate (a locked account can't open a subscription), but the per-notification/per-ping predicate session_alive (server/src/ws.rs) re-checks only token-resolves + share-ACL(Read) + vault-exists — NOT users.must_change(user). So an account that transitions to must_change WHILE a WS is open isn't torn down by that dimension alone. NOT a live defect: every path that sets must_change on an account with a live token ALSO revokes tokens (admin reset set_must_change+revoke_user; self-service change_password revoke_user; admin-create sets it on a token-less new account), and session_alive catches the paired revoke via token_ok==false -> teardown. So the socket does tear down, transitively through token revocation, not through the flag. FRAGILE INVARIANT (relies on callers pairing set_must_change with revoke_user), not a current leak. FIX (backlog, wsSessionAliveMustCheckBacklog): add must_change to session_alive so the liveness check is self-contained (defense-in-depth). Requires a server redeploy; low priority (not exploitable). ALSO from the same hunt, NOT tracked as separate Issues (accepted tradeoffs / negligible, not defects): the per-delivery re-auth (mutex + fs-stat) amplification on high fan-out (a deliberate prompt-revocation security-vs-scale tradeoff); a bounded cosmetic transient false-green over a not-yet-detected half-open socket (backstopped by the 60s idle poll + onResume); and a shared lastWsActivity field across socket generations (sub-ms window, negligible). discoveredInField=false (WS-hunt-found). Severity Low."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "wsSessionAliveMustCheckBacklog"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from wsSessionAliveMustCheckBacklog to issueWsSessionAliveMustChange;

    // Owner-directed 2026-08-03: 'revisit the timestamp area UX. descriptions are way too verbose, and the
    // date field identification should be an array input, not a huge text field. we try and prevent errors.'
    part issueTimestampSettingsVerboseUx : Issue { :>> id = "17ea5b96-4f13-4d80-b6a2-5c1e0f286d40"; :>> title = "The timestamp settings are too verbose and the ignored-date-fields input is a huge free-text field (no error prevention)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-03"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-directed UX cleanup of the 'Timestamp changes' settings (settings.ts renderIgnoreTimestamps): the descriptions were paragraph-length, and 'Ignored date fields' was a 5-row TEXTAREA (one-key-per-line free text) — a malformed / duplicate / unmatchable key just got silently accepted. FIX (timestampSettingsUx 1.12.7, client-only): (1) tightened all three descriptions (master toggle, date fields, excluded folders) to one short line; (2) replaced the textarea with a validated ADD-a-row LIST mirroring the excluded-folders + plugin-checklist pattern — each key is its own row with a Remove button, plus an 'Add a date field' row whose 'Add field' button runs a pure validateTimestampKey poka-yoke (rejects empty / a ':' that would break the YAML key / an unmatchable shape / a case-insensitive duplicate, each with a short Notice) so a bad entry is PREVENTED rather than accepted; (3) a 'Restore default date fields' safety shown only when the list has drifted (so a user can't get stranded with an emptied list — the old textarea's implicit empty->defaults is now explicit). No reconcile-semantics change (same s.ignoredTimestampKeys + saveSettings as the textarea). Guards: frontmatter.test validateTimestampKey (accepts the default shapes/wildcards/spaces, trims, rejects empty/colon/junk/case-dup); settings-ui.dom 'date fields are a validated add/remove LIST' + 'an INVALID or DUPLICATE date field is REJECTED'. discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Low (UX / error-prevention)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "timestampSettingsUx"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from timestampSettingsUx to issueTimestampSettingsVerboseUx;

    // Owner-directed 2026-08-03 (follow-up + screenshot): little-X removes, a default-collapsible timestamp
    // section, move Reconfigure/Disconnect up, brief store-password, and 'the "X synced" collapse looks
    // really bad' — 'research canonical ways ... don't reinvent'.
    part issueSettingsUxPolish : Issue { :>> id = "28fb6ca7-5024-4f91-c7b3-16ad04f13bb5"; :>> title = "Settings UX rough edges: verbose store-password, huge Remove buttons, an orphaned/ugly collapse widget, buried Reconfigure/Disconnect"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-03"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-directed settings polish (with a mobile screenshot): (1) the per-row 'Remove' buttons on the date-field / excluded-folder lists were huge — replace with a little X; the lists are easily restorable so no scary warning; (2) the Timestamp-changes section should be COLLAPSIBLE (default collapsed — not seen most of the time); (3) the synced-plugin list's collapse rendered as an ORPHANED raw <details> disclosure triangle floating between cards ('looks really bad ... must be a better way'); the owner directed 'research canonical ways ... don't reinvent'; (4) move the Reconfigure/Disconnect item UP from Advanced into the top Connection area; (5) the store-password description was a wall of text — make it brief per human factors. FIX (settingsUxPolish 1.12.8, client-only): little-X via Obsidian's canonical addExtraButton().setIcon('x') (confirmed the canonical icon-action idiom); a collapsible() helper built from Obsidian's OWN documented components — Setting().setHeading() (the canonical section-heading, per the API docs) + a native lucide chevron via setIcon() + a body toggle — NOT a custom disclosure widget (researched: Obsidian has no native collapsible-group API, SettingGroup v1.11.0 has no collapse; setHeading + native chevron is the community idiom); used for BOTH the plugin list (fixing the orphaned triangle) and the default-collapsed Timestamp section; Reconfigure/Disconnect moved into fillStatus (top Connection group); store-password desc cut to one line. Guards: settings-ui.dom 'the Timestamp changes section is a collapsible (default collapsed) that toggles on header click' + the existing timestamp add/remove-list tests (the little-X extra-button is clickable); obsidian-stub gained ExtraButtonComponent + addExtraButton. discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Low (UX)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "settingsUxPolish"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from settingsUxPolish to issueSettingsUxPolish;

    // Owner-reported 2026-08-03 (screenshot, on 1.12.8): 'dropdown looks the same, not like a heading' — the
    // 1.12.8 collapse header didn't render as a heading.
    part issueCollapseHeaderNotHeading : Issue { :>> id = "4abd8ec9-7246-4d13-e9d7-38cf26135df8"; :>> title = "The collapsible section header (1.12.8) rendered as a plain small row, not a heading — setHeading() was called without setName()"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-03"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Follow-up to settingsUxPolish (1.12.8). The collapsible() helper called new Setting(c).setHeading() but built the label from a MANUAL createSpan instead of setName(title), so Obsidian's heading styling (which targets the .setting-item-name text) never applied — the 'N synced' / 'Timestamp changes' collapse headers rendered as small dim rows, not headings (owner screenshot: 'dropdown looks the same, not like a heading'). FIX (collapseHeaderFix 1.12.9, client-only): use the canonical new Setting(c).setName(title).setHeading() (styles the name as a heading), insert the native lucide chevron BEFORE the title (section-collapse convention), + a styles.css safety (.selfsync-collapse-header .setting-item-name { font-weight: 600 }) so it reads as a heading even on themes that render .setting-item-heading subtly. Guard: settings-ui.dom 'the Timestamp changes section is a collapsible ...' still green (header found by text + toggles). discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Low (UX)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "collapseHeaderFix"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from collapseHeaderFix to issueCollapseHeaderNotHeading;
    part issueCollapseStillNotHeadingMobile : Issue { :>> id = "7de0fb1c-a579-4e46-0af0-61fb48368a0b"; :>> title = "The 1.12.9 setName().setHeading() collapse header STILL rendered as a small dim row (not a heading) on mobile — an inline setting-item-heading is styled far more subtly than the SettingGroup section headings"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-03"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Follow-up to collapseHeaderFix (1.12.9). The owner hard-restarted Obsidian (confirming 1.12.9 was actually running, ruling out a stale-plugin/mobile-restart cause) and reported the '7 synced' collapse STILL looked the same — a small dim row, not a section heading like 'Synced community plugins'/'Advanced'. Root cause: 1.12.8/1.12.9 built the header from Setting().setHeading() — an inline heading ROW (.setting-item-heading) — but the prominent section headings the owner compares against are SettingGroup headings, which mobile themes style much more prominently; no setName()/font-weight tweak could match them. FIX (collapseHeaderGroupFix 1.12.10, client-only): render the collapsible() header as a real new SettingGroup(c).setHeading(title) — the SAME component as the other section headings — and toggle the group's public listEl as the body (heading reached via listEl.previousElementSibling; chevron prepended). Guard: settings-ui.dom collapse test updated to the SettingGroup heading + listEl toggle (green). discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Low (UX)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "collapseHeaderGroupFix"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from collapseHeaderGroupFix to issueCollapseStillNotHeadingMobile;
    part issuePluginWiringTimerLeak : Issue { :>> id = "8ef1c2b0-5d3a-4f61-b0c2-91fe4d2a7b30"; :>> title = "plugin-wiring.test.ts intermittently failed 'D0047 guard is SERVER-qualified' — a leaked reconnect/poll window-timer from a prior test fired mid-assertion and re-stamped baseVaultKey (test-isolation flake)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-03"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Recurring flake (seen ~1 in 3 full-suite runs across the 1.12.6 and 1.13.0 sittings): the full client vitest reported '1 failed' in 'D0047 guard is SERVER-qualified' (plugin-wiring), always green in isolation and on re-run. Root cause: bootPlugin() schedules REAL Node window.setTimeout/setInterval (a reconnect backoff or a poll interval); if a test's plugin.onunload() misses one, that timer persists across tests (real timers aren't tied to the per-test window object) and fires during a LATER test, driving a stray reconnect that re-stamps settings.baseVaultKey mid-assertion. FIX (pluginWiringTimerLeakFix): TRACK every window timer a test schedules and force-clear all live ones in an afterEach — guaranteeing timer isolation regardless of whether every onunload path cleared its own timers (D0047: a correction becomes a permanent guard, here a test-harness guard). Verified by 3 consecutive full-suite runs green. Severity Low (test reliability, not product)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "pluginWiringTimerLeakFix"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from pluginWiringTimerLeakFix to issuePluginWiringTimerLeak;
    part issueCollapseChevronDetached : Issue { :>> id = "9f0a2d31-6e4b-4c72-a1d3-b2e5f60c8a41"; :>> title = "The 1.12.10 SettingGroup collapse placed its chevron by guessing the heading DOM (listEl.previousElementSibling) — wrong on mobile Obsidian, so the chevron rendered detached inside the list card and the label didn't toggle"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-03"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Follow-up to collapseHeaderGroupFix (1.12.10). Making the collapse header a real SettingGroup heading fixed its APPEARANCE, but the chevron + click were placed by guessing the heading element via group.listEl.previousElementSibling. That guess is wrong on mobile Obsidian's SettingGroup DOM: the chevron got inserted into the list container (rendered at the top-left of the card, divorced from the '7 synced' label) and the click bound to the wrong element, so tapping the label did nothing (owner screenshot: 'the X synced label isn't actually associated with the dropdown'). FIX (collapseChevronFix 1.14.1, client-only): add the chevron via addExtraButton — the DOCUMENTED way to put a control in a SettingGroup heading — so it lands IN the heading next to the label with no DOM guessing; the whole heading row toggles, derived from the button via the stable .setting-item class; degrades gracefully (the chevron button itself toggles even if the row-click derivation fails). obsidian-stub updated to model SettingGroup.addExtraButton in the heading. Guard: settings-ui.dom collapse test green. discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Low (UX)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "collapseChevronFix"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from collapseChevronFix to issueCollapseChevronDetached;
    part issueConnectionRowButtonOverflow : Issue { :>> id = "b1c2d3e4-6f70-4a81-b2c3-d4e5f6a7b8c9"; :>> title = "1.15.0 Connection Server row: the long server URL sat inline with the Setup/Disconnect buttons and pushed Disconnect off the card on mobile"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-04"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported on 1.15.0 (mobile screenshot): the Server fact row rendered the value (a long https:// URL) as an inline control-area span alongside the Setup + Disconnect buttons; on a narrow screen the URL consumed the width and pushed 'Disconnect' past the card's right edge. Short values (username, vault name) fit, so only the long-URL Server row overflowed. FIX (connectionRowOverflowFix 1.15.1, client-only): factRow renders each fact's value as the row DESCRIPTION (full-width, wraps) instead of an inline value competing with the buttons — the control row then holds only the action buttons; robust for any long value. Retired the unused .selfsync-value CSS. Guard: settings-ui.dom green. discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Low (UX)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "connectionRowOverflowFix"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from connectionRowOverflowFix to issueConnectionRowButtonOverflow;
    part issuePluginDirectionInert : Issue { :>> id = "c2d3e4f5-7081-4b92-c3d4-e5f6a7b8c9d0"; :>> title = "The per-plugin config-sync direction dropdown ('Use server's'/'Use this device's') was INERT for an already-synced plugin — it only governed a no-base first contact, so flipping it did nothing, silently (an honest-status / L-5 gap)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-04"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-identified: the per-plugin first-contact direction dropdown implied a live 'which copy wins' switch, but configAutoResolveChoice only consults the direction when a divergence has NO common base (reason=='conflict-copy'). An already-synced plugin HAS a shared base, so its ongoing changes reconcile via merge/edit-wins and the direction is never used — flipping the dropdown changed nothing, with no feedback. That is exactly the misleading-UX the nHonestStatus Need (STPA L-5) is about. FIX (pluginPushPull 1.16.0, client-only, owner chose option #1): replace the dropdown (for an installed+synced plugin on a read-write vault) with explicit PUSH (make the server + other devices match this device's copy) / PULL (replace this device's copy with the server's) actions — confirmed authoritative overwrites scoped to .obsidian/plugins/<id>/, over the union of local+server files (pluginFilePaths), reusing the tested resolveConfigConflict. Independent critique run: hardened the self-folder guard to the shared case-insensitive+legacy isSelfPluginId (forcePlugin bypasses shouldSync) and made partial-failure reported not swallowed. Guards: pluginFilePaths + isSelfPluginId pure tests + settings-ui.dom Push/Pull wiring; full client vitest 589. discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Low (UX/behavior)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "pluginPushPull"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from pluginPushPull to issuePluginDirectionInert;
    part issueDeletionProvenanceUnnotified : Issue { :>> id = "d3e4f5a6-8192-4ca3-d4e5-f6a7b8c9d0e1"; :>> title = "Source-driven config-change notifications (1.18.0) don't cover a peer's config/plugin DELETION — the Deletion wire type carries no provenance"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-05"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus"; :>> description = "Surfaced by the independent security critique of changeProvenanceNotify (1.18.0, finding 6). The new source-driven reload notice attributes and notifies on ADD/EDIT config changes (each carries author/device provenance via FileMeta), but a peer DELETING a synced config file, CSS snippet, or plugin folder is applied on this device with NO notice: io.remove never marks pendingReload, and the Deletion protocol record ({path, version}) has no author/device fields to attribute it anyway. Partly pre-existing (deletions were never notified — there was no source-notification before this feature), so NOT a regression; it's a coverage boundary of the new capability. A follow-up (deletionProvenanceNotify) would add provenance to the server's Deletion record + a delete-path notice keyed on the same shouldNotifyConfigChange rule. discoveredInField=false (critique-found). Severity Low (a missed notice on a rarer event; no data risk — the deletion itself still reconciles correctly)."; :>> discoveredInField = false; :>> relatedTask = "deletionProvenanceNotify"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from deletionProvenanceNotify to issueDeletionProvenanceUnnotified;
    part issueSettingsScrollResetsOnToggle : Issue { :>> id = "e4f5a6b7-9203-4db4-e5f6-a7b8c9d0e1f2"; :>> title = "Toggling a setting (e.g. a community-plugin checkbox) resets the settings page scroll to the TOP"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-05"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported: 'most of the time when I click an option, a slide toggle on community plugins for example, it resets the settings page to the top.' Root cause: many settings handlers (the config-sync surface toggles, the community-plugin checklist) call this.display() to reflect the new state, and display() does containerEl.empty() + a full rebuild of the whole tab — which discards the scroll position, jumping the page to the top. The Status hero already avoids this (FSM ticks refresh JUST its row via fillStatus, per the display() comment), but the toggle handlers do a whole-tab rebuild. FIX (settingsScrollPreserve 1.21.1, client-only): preserve scroll ACROSS the rebuild in ONE place — display() captures the scrolling ancestor's scrollTop before empty() (via scrollEl(), walking up for the overflow:auto/scroll ancestor) and restores it after (sections rebuild at the same heights). Centralised in display() so EVERY re-render is covered structurally, not by per-handler vigilance (D0047: the control is the single choke point, not manual care). Not unit-testable in happy-dom (no layout → no real scroll), so verified by reasoning + on-device; the structural centralisation IS the durable guard. discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Low (UX — no data risk)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "settingsScrollPreserve"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from settingsScrollPreserve to issueSettingsScrollResetsOnToggle;
    part issuePushPullDisabledStillClickable : Issue { :>> id = "f5a6b7c8-a304-4ec5-f6a7-b8c9d0e1f2a3"; :>> title = "The 1.21.2 'disabled' Push/Pull buttons were greyed but STILL CLICKABLE — setDisabled doesn't block clicks on an extra-button"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-05"; :>> createdBy = "wweatherholtz"; :>> description = "Owner-reported on 1.21.2 (pushPullDisableInactionable): 'the push and pull buttons are grayed, but clickable. they should be disabled/unclickable.' Root cause: ExtraButtonComponent.setDisabled only adds an is-disabled CLASS to the button's div/anchor (it is NOT a native <button disabled>), so it greys the control but does NOT prevent the registered onClick from firing; the act() guard made the click a no-op, but the button still FELT interactive (hover / cursor / click feedback). This was the exact 'setDisabled doesn't block the click' caveat the earlier grey-out critique had flagged, resurfaced when the owner reversed the hint to a hard disable. FIX (pushPullDisableInert 1.21.3, client-only): when converged, ALSO set pointer-events:none on the button element, making it genuinely INERT (no click, no hover) — a real disabled control; the act() guard stays as defense-in-depth. GUARD (D0047): the settings-ui.dom test now asserts the converged button's style.pointerEvents === 'none' (not merely that a click no-ops), so a regression back to greyed-but-clickable is caught automatically. discoveredInField=true (owner). Severity Low (UX)."; :>> discoveredInField = true; :>> relatedTask = "pushPullDisableInert"; :>> severity = Severity::Low; }
    #Resolves dependency from pushPullDisableInert to issuePushPullDisabledStillClickable;
}
